notes.text:
Headlines must catch attention in very few words, so every word is chosen for impact. Headline writers use:

• **strong, dramatic verbs**: “slams”, “blasts”, “axes”, “storms”, “plunges”;
• **short, punchy nouns**: “row” (argument), “probe” (investigation), “bid” (attempt), “chaos”;
• **the present tense** for past events, to make news feel immediate (“Minister quits”);
• **omission** of small words such as articles and auxiliary verbs (“Five hurt in N1 crash”);
• **puns, alliteration and rhyme** to entertain (“Boks to the future”);
• **question headlines** that suggest something without having to prove it.

The connotations of the words in a headline often reveal the publication's attitude before you have read a single sentence of the article.
